From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wineps.drv, pscript5, ps5ui, gdi32, winprint, winspool: a printer maker's PostScript driver is our PostScript driver with the maker's PPD

Office printers' PostScript drivers (HP, Xerox, Ricoh, Kyocera, Konica,
Brother BR-Script, Toshiba...) are a PPD on the system's PostScript driver:
the INF needs PSCRIPT.OEM from NTPRINT.INF and names PSCRIPT5.DLL and
PS5UI.DLL; some makers ship copies of those. Such a package did not
install, and nothing printed. Now:

- pscript5.dll and ps5ui.dll (new) are ours and lead to wineps.drv, Wine's
  PostScript driver, which reads the maker's PPD (the driver's data file);
  a printer DC of such a driver is wineps's, and winprint plays its jobs
  through wineps's print processor.
- wineps offers and sends every option the PPD describes (*OpenUI and
  *JCLOpenUI groups), not only paper, tray and duplex: the choices live in
  the devmode after wineps's tables; dmColor, dmCollate, dmMediaType and
  dmPrintQuality choose the matching options (ColorModel and other colour
  keywords, Collate, MediaType, Resolution) and read back from them; each
  option's code goes where its *OrderDependency puts it (Prolog,
  DocumentSetup/AnySetup in its order, PageSetup on every page).
- A maker's driver's job is the printer's: it starts with the PPD's
  *JCLBegin, the JCL options and *JCLToPSInterpreter and ends with
  *JCLEnd, and asks for the copies (NumCopies). A CUPS printer's PPD keeps
  its behaviour: only the options that differ from the defaults are sent.
- DeviceCapabilities answers collation, stapling and media types from the
  PPD.

Brother's BR-Script3 package prints valid, colour PostScript with its PJL
and options.

Test: test/psdriver-gate.sh, 64- and 32-bit (mutants SG_MUTANT_PSCORE,
PPDUI, PPDWRITE, PSJOB).
